[0.008, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Did our signups fall from last month?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: low\r\nTook 71s over 6 steps, 26,566 tokens, 17 observations gathered, 12 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  No — signups have not fallen from last month; the daily signup rate has stayed essentially flat, around 150–164 per day, through both July and the available August data.  [posthog.event_trend 245d493d]\r\n  A GA4 report comparing full July to full August shows a 61.9% drop in sessions, but this is an artifact of comparing a complete 31-day July against only 12 days of recorded August data, not a real decline — daily rates in both months are comparable.  [ga4.compare_periods d78c7607, ga4.get_sessions e547ea32]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Product signups (pos"]
[0.000, "o", "thog 'user signed up') are flat, not falling  (medium)\r\n    - Daily signup counts from PostHog were consistently in the 149–164 range every day from July 1 through August 12, 2026, with no visible decline in the second period.  [posthog.event_trend 245d493d]\r\n  The GA4 comparison that looks like a large drop is comparing a full month to a partial month  (medium)\r\n    - GA4's month-over-month comparison shows sessions down 61.9% (4,850 to 1,848) and conversions down 61.81% (199 to 76) for July vs August, but the underlying GA4 daily sessions data only extends through August 12, so the August figure represents roughly 12 days against July's full 31 days.  [ga4.compare_periods d78c7607, ga4.get_sessions e547ea32]\r\n    - Comparing daily rates instead of totals, GA4 sessions in the first 12 days of August average around 153/day, essentially the same as July's daily average of about 157/day, so the apparent drop is a length-of-period artifact rather than a real decline.  [ga4.get_sessions e547ea32]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n---"]
[0.000, "o", "---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |▄▁█▇▅▃█▅▇▇▅▄▅▄▂▃▇▁▁▅▃▅▄▃█▂▄▂▅▃▃▇▃▅█▂▁▃▇▆▃▃▂|  range 149–164\r\n                       |                                  ^        |\r\n      ^1 Pricing page redesign to 100% (2026-08-04)\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n    sessions           |▄▁▅▇█▆█▁▃█▆▄▇▅▆▃▂▄▂▄█▃▁▁▂▆▃▃▁█▄▂▁▁▂▆▂▁▅▃█▂▅|  range 149–165\r\n                       |                                  ^        |\r\n      ^1 Pricing page redesign to 100% (2026-08-04)\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - GA4 and PostHog data for August both stop at August 12, so the true full-month August figures "]
[0.000, "o", "are not available; if activity dropped sharply between August 13 and August 31 (a period we cannot see), the flat-rate conclusion would not hold for the full month.\r\n  - A pricing page redesign shipped August 4, within the visible window, but signup and session rates before and after that date show no shift, so it does not appear to explain anything — this is noted only because it was raised as a concern, not because it changed the answer.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - Both PostHog event data and GA4 session data are only available through 2026-08-12, well short of the full August window through the report date of 2026-09-23. All August comparisons in this report use daily rates over that partial window rather than month totals.\r\n  - GA4 breakdowns by channel and landing page"]
[0.000, "o", " returned no rows for the comparison window, so a channel-level view of the (illusory) drop could not be produced.\r\n  - GitHub commit and PR history for acme/product and acme/company-website returned empty for the mid-July through August window, and no Slack decision record or feature note on a 'pricing redesign' was found beyond the one PostHog annotation, so a deploy-based explanation could not be independently corroborated.\r\n  - 8 of the 12 cited observation(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, github.commits, github.find_feature, github.recent_prs, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  d4cf47c6  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  d78c7607  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  52eec14d  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  e547ea32  ga4.get_sessions  e"]
